**Diagnosis:** `parsePort` used `Number.parseInt(value)`. `parseInt` stops reading at the first invalid character, so `'8080abc'` became `8080` — an apparently valid port — and no exception was thrown. `test.js`'s "rejects trailing junk" case therefore failed with `Missing expected exception`.

**Fix (src.js only):** validate the *whole* input against `/^\d+$/` before converting, then range-check the resulting number:

```js
export function parsePort(value) {
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(String(value))) throw new Error("invalid port")
  const port = Number(value)
  if (!Number.isInteger(port) || port < 1 || port > 65535) throw new Error("invalid port")
  return port
}
```

Using `String(value)` keeps the old tolerance for numeric input (e.g. `8080`), while `'8080abc'`, `''`, and `' 8080'` now throw.

